    "narrative": "For today\u2019s slate, **Kyle Tucker** (72.07) and **Vladimir Guerrero Jr.** (67.70) stand as our elite anchor points. **Kyle Tucker** benefits from the most favorable pitching matchup on the board, facing Phillips (4.52 xERA), and his ability to leverage that weak arm against a right-handed starter cements his position at the top of our lineup. **Vladimir Guerrero Jr.** similarly profiles as a high-floor play despite a moderate test against Bradish; his elevated xwOBA and favorable spot in the order allow him to overcome a slight platoon disadvantage. While **Gunnar Henderson** and **Heliot Ramos** fall into our \"Strong\" start tier, both provide high-ceiling utility as they look to exploit their respective matchups, with **Gunnar Henderson** maintaining a solid projection despite a slightly tougher SP skill draw from Miles.\n\nOur most difficult roster decision involved keeping **Coby Mayo** (38.12) on the bench. While his underlying power metrics remain attractive, his matchup against Miles (3.20 xERA) triggered a significant discount in the optimizer, ultimately falling just short of the 40.0 floor required to justify a start in our game-cap-conscious environment. Elsewhere, **Jackson Merrill** presents a localized risk; while his base projection is strong, the -13% hit from the platoon disadvantage against Perdomo is only partially mitigated by wind-out conditions, making him a volatile piece in today\u2019s configuration. \n\nA final note of caution surrounds **Drake Baldwin**, whose score of 48.33 is predicated on a \"Lineup Pending\" assumption of the leadoff spot. If **Drake Baldwin** shifts lower in the order, his utility diminishes rapidly. Furthermore, we continue to monitor the status of **Luis Robert Jr.** and **Luis Garc\u00eda Jr.**, who remain sidelined; their absence remains a frustration as we attempt to maximize our stolen base output, a category that is currently straining under the weight of our current active roster constraints.",
